+                # and concatenate them to the current batch for the next iteration.
+                # This step is complex to fully detail without external state.
+                pass
+
+    return nelf
 ```
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Trainability of Deep Networks:** Deep networks for NeRF/NeLF suffer from diminishing returns if not designed carefully. Residual connections are critical; without them, the network is barely trainable [S1].
-*   **Overfitting:** Deep networks require abundant data. Training on only original images (typically <100) leads to severe overfitting (e.g., 19.81 PSNR vs 26.67 with pseudo data) [S1].
-*   **Hard Example Mining:** Without hard example mining, the network may focus on trivial parts of the scene (e.g., white background) and fail to learn fine-grained details [S1].
-*   **Reflection Surfaces:** Unlike methods relying on ground-truth depth (e.g., DONeRF), NeLF does not fail on reflective surfaces (water, mirrors) because it does not use depth supervision [S1].
+*   **Trainability of Deep Networks:** Without residual connections, the deep network (88 layers) is barely trainable [S1].
+*   **Overfitting:** Deep networks require abundant data. Using only original captured images (typically <100) leads to severe overfitting (e.g., 19.81 PSNR on Lego) [S1]. Pseudo data is critical [S1].
+*   **Input Representation:** Concatenated coordinates perform better than Plücker coordinates (PSNR 29.50 vs 29.08 on Lego) [S1].
+*   **Reflection Surfaces:** Unlike methods relying on ground-truth depth (e.g., DONeRF), NeLF handles reflective surfaces (water, mirrors) well because it does not depend on depth maps which fail on reflections [S1].
